{
"Conventional truth is like money: it exists by agreement, and it is completely real in what it does.": "433e442fec3a",
"In English conventional suggests something arbitrary, and ultimate suggests something higher and better. Together they tell the listener that Buddhism has a real truth and a pretend one, and that serious people concern themselves with the first. Start by repairing conventional. Money is the best example. A banknote is paper, and its value exists only because people treat it as valuable. No one concludes that money is unreal or that it does not matter whether you have any. Its value is conventional and entirely real in its effects. Persons, promises, and nations are like that. Then say what the ultimate truth is: the fact that this is how such things exist. It is the same things, correctly understood.": "8a29ac5fd6eb",
"Kant held that space, time, and the order of causes belong to the way our minds take in the world. Within experience they are fully real. He maintained the empirical reality of space in regard to all possible external experience, although we must admit its transcendental ideality, that is, that it is nothing, so soon as we withdraw the condition upon which the possibility of all experience depends. This is close to the Madhyamaka formula: conventionally existent, ultimately empty. Both deny that the world as we find it exists in its own right, and both refuse to call it an illusion. They separate over the second level. Kant placed a thing in itself behind appearances and said that its nature is quite unknown to us. Nāgārjuna placed nothing behind them. He held that the ultimate can be known directly, and that what is known is the absence of anything standing behind.": "89c25c2bfc38",
"Kant said the world is real for experience and not real in itself. Nāgārjuna agreed, and added that nothing is hidden behind it.": "a6d8e3cee80e",
"Nāgārjuna kept the two truths and changed what the second one is. The Buddhas teach the Dharma by relying on two truths: the conventional truth of the world, and the truth of ultimate meaning. Those who do not understand the distinction between the two truths do not understand the real meaning of the profound teaching. His analysis did not stop at the parts. The parts are empty too, and no final layer of real things is reached. The ultimate truth about a chariot is that neither it nor its parts have own-nature. So the ultimate is no second world behind this one. It is the way this one is. He added a rule about order: Without relying on the conventional, the ultimate cannot be attained. Without attaining the ultimate, nirvana cannot be attained. The commentary gives the reason. The ultimate has to be spoken of, and all speech is conventional.": "62c690216de9",
"Nāgārjuna said that you cannot reach the ultimate without relying on the conventional. Ethics is where the path starts, and it is never left behind.": "6080846bf8d1",
"The Buddha taught that there is no self, and he still said I. The two truths explain how both are right.": "a14658543013",
"The Western traditions first and most influential two-level picture is Platos. There is the changing world that the senses show us, and there is the world of Forms that reason grasps, and the second is more real. The prisoners in the cave take shadows for things, and the philosopher climbs out to see the originals. Many Western readers assume that the Buddhist two truths are a version of this, with emptiness or nirvana in the place of the Forms and ordinary life as the shadows. The structure is reversed. Platos higher level contains the things that most fully exist, and each has a fixed nature. Nāgārjunas ultimate truth is that nothing has such a nature, and it is a truth about the things of this world, with no other location. A student who leaves the cave in Platos story arrives somewhere else. One who understands the two truths has not gone anywhere.": "1de651437bf6",
"The early schools turned this into a doctrine of two kinds of statement. Some of the Buddhas words describe things as they are finally analyzed: aggregates, sense bases, moments of feeling. Others use the names of wholes: a person, a chariot, an army. Statements of the second kind are true by convention. They are useful and correct in their place, and what they name can be resolved into the items of the first kind. The Buddha himself warned against confusing the two registers. A person misrepresents him, he said, who explains a discourse in need of interpretation as a discourse whose meaning is explicit, or the reverse. On this version the ultimate is what is left when analysis is finished.": "938f2e101d61",
"The first further step is the rule of order. Western two-level views generally treat the lower level as something to get beyond: out of the cave, behind the appearances, down to the particles. Nāgārjuna says that the ultimate is reached only by relying on the conventional, and the reason is practical. The path is made of conventional things: words, teachers, precepts, kindness to particular people. A student who announces that conduct is merely conventional and so does not matter has misunderstood both truths at once. The teaching anticipates him. Because actions and results hold conventionally, and conventional holding is the only kind there is, they hold in the fullest sense available. The lessons on emptiness and on the four corners reached the same point by other routes.": "46468bee49de",
"The likeness to Kant ends at three points. He has an unknowable something behind appearances, and Nāgārjuna has no such thing. He thinks the human mind is fixed in its forms and cannot see otherwise, and the Buddhist path is a training by which the ultimate comes to be seen. His two standpoints are a result in the theory of knowledge, and the two truths are a teaching device with a use. The Buddha spoke of persons to people who needed to hear about conduct and consequence, and of aggregates and emptiness to those ready to look into what a person is. The purpose of keeping both is that neither nihilism nor attachment to things follows. Kant wanted to show what we can know. Nāgārjuna wanted a way of speaking truthfully to everyone without confirming anyones clinging.": "97034dd12dec",
"The physicist Arthur Eddington began a famous book by saying that he was writing at two tables: the solid, colored one of everyday life and the scientific one, mostly empty space with electric charges rushing about. The American philosopher Wilfrid Sellars made the contrast into a problem for philosophy. We have a manifest image of the world, in which there are persons who think, intend, and are responsible, and a scientific image, in which there are particles and fields. The task is to see how both can be held together. This matches the early Buddhist two truths almost exactly. The chariot and the person belong to the manifest image, and the ultimately real items are the ones a finished analysis would list. Sellars and the Abhidharma also agree that persons belong to the first image, and that this does not make talk of persons dispensable. They differ from Nāgārjuna in the same way. Sellars held that in describing what there is, science is the measure. Nāgārjuna held that the scientific image, like any other, is a conventional truth, and that no description reaches things with own-nature.": "e291c0e93bf1",
"The problem appears in the earliest texts. The Buddha taught that no self is to be found, and he went on using the words everyone uses. A deity asked whether a monk who has reached the goal would still say I speak and they speak to me. He would, the Buddha answered. Skillful, understanding the worlds labels, theyd use these terms as mere expressions. Elsewhere, after listing the ordinary names for things, he says: These are the worlds common usages, definitions, expressions, and descriptions, which the Realized One uses to communicate without getting stuck on them. The nun Vajirā gave the classic image when Māra tried to unsettle her with questions about who made this being. When the parts are assembled we use the word chariot. So too, when the aggregates are present sentient being is the convention we use.": "3b8ad2071ff3",
"The second step is that the two truths collapse the distance between the goal and the starting point. If the ultimate is the emptiness of this world and no other realm, then awakening is a change in how this world is seen. Nāgārjuna states the consequence in a verse that startled his readers: Between nirvana and the world there is not the slightest difference. Between the world and nirvana there is not the slightest difference. The commentary explains that both are empty and unarisen, so that no line can be drawn between them. He does not mean that ordinary confusion is already awakening. The Chinese and Japanese schools built much on this verse, and it gave the Mahāyāna its characteristic confidence that practice is done in the middle of ordinary life. It also explains why the bodhisattva does not leave. On Platos picture the one who gets out has a reason not to come back. On this one there is nowhere else to be.": "109de0c5b5cd",
"The ultimate truth is not about another world. It is about how this one exists.": "587f4bf88482",
"Two Buddhist doctrines go by the name of the two truths, and each has its own nearest neighbor. The early version, in which wholes are conventional and their final constituents ultimate, is closest to Sellars and to scientific reduction generally. Any modern reader who says that a table is really atoms holds a two-truths view of that kind. The Madhyamaka version is closest to Kant, for whom the world is real for experience and not real in itself. Plato, who is usually the first comparison to come to mind, is the furthest of the three.": "26172f282bf0",
"When someone says thats only conventional, ask what else there is for a thing to be.": "745ef489d607"
}
